	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: BOS is the obstructive phenotype of chronic lung allograft dysfunction, so the closest restricted category is CLAD. The main result was aggregated ROC-AUC 0.90 (95% CI 0.840–0.953) for BOS versus non-BOS. Performance by stage was 0.88, 0.91, and 0.94 for BOS stages I, II, and III, and 0.87 for at-risk patients with FEV1 80–90% of best. The paper did not report accuracy, PPV, sensitivity, specificity, NPV, F1-score, calibration, or DCA.  ￼

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: This was a small single-center cohort of 75 patients, with only 26 BOS cases, and additional scan exclusions were applied, which raises selection concerns.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors were chest CT scans processed with a defined DNN pipeline, and CT acquisition/reconstruction details were explicitly described.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: BOS was defined according to “current diagnostic guidelines,” with onset defined when FEV1 consistently fell below 80% of best FEV1.  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include patient-level five-fold cross-validation, bootstrapped confidence intervals, stage-specific evaluation, and at-risk detection analysis. Uncertainty remains because this is a low-data, single-center study without external validation.  ￼
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly addresses AI-based BOS detection after lung transplantation using CT imaging and is highly relevant to the review question.  ￼

